  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe are tackling the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem: finding a function `h: [0,2] -> [0,1]` with integral 0.5 that minimizes the maximum overlap with its time-shifted complement. The previous implementation uses simulated annealing starting from a parent solution. \n\nThe key bottleneck is that simulated annealing may not escape local optima. Our new approach uses a genetic algorithm (GA) with tournament selection and simulated binary crossover (SBX) combined with polynomial mutation. This leverages global exploration capabilities for improved results.\n\nThe GA will:\n1. Maintain a population of potential `h` functions\n2. Apply selection, crossover, and mutation through repair to enforce constraints\n3. Evaluate fitness using autocorrelation via FFT\n4. Track the best solution\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Representation: `h` is a vector of length `n_points`, with entries in [0,1] and summed to `n_points/4` (due to `dx=2/n_points`)\n- Objective: Minimize `C5 = max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx`\n- Autocorrelation calculation: Use FFT-based correlation with mode 'full', then find the maximum and multiply by `dx`\n- Repair: Ensure non-negativity, integral constraint, and clipping at [0,1]\n- Fitness: Lower `C5` values are preferred\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n- Parameters:\n  - Population size: 50\n  - Mutation rate: 0.1\n  - Crossover parameter: eta=10\n  - Tournament size: 3\n\n- Steps:\n  - Initialize population with parent solution (if available) or random\n  - Evaluate fitness for each candidate\n  - While time remaining:\n    - Find two parents via tournament selection\n    - Apply SBX crossover\n    - Apply polynomial mutation\n    - Repair the offspring\n    - Compute `C5`\n    - Update best solution if `C5` improves\n  - Return best solution, its `C5`, and `n_points`\n\n4. Search Choices\n- Fitness evaluation:\n  - FFT-based overlap calculation\n  - Avoid direct correlation between parent and offspring\n\n- Scaling rules:\n  - Integral constraint: `sum(h)*dx = 0.5`\n  - Bounds: `0 <= h[i] <= 1`\n\n- Termination:\n  - Max evaluations: 10,000\n  - Time budget: 100 seconds\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Time estimation:\n  - Repair: O(n log n) for sorting\n  - FFT: O(n log n) per candidate\n  - Param 280: ~15 ms per evaluation\n  - Full run: ~20-50,000 evaluations\n\n- Response to failures:\n  - Track best solution continuously\n  - Ensure repairs maintain constraints\n  - Use backup repair mechanism\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Requirements:\n  1. `sum(h) * dx = 0.5`\n  2. `0 <= h[i] <= 1`\n  3. `C5 <= target` (0.380876)\n\n- Validation:\n  - Repair ensures constraints\n  - Craft fallback solution if needed\n  - Final candidate sent to evaluator\n\nSpecific Implementation Steps:\n\n1. Run setup:\n  - Set `n_points = 280`\n  - `dx = 2.0 / n_points`\n  - `integral_target = n_points / 4`\n\n2. Repair function:\n  - Compute weights: `1/abs(idx-center)+1e-6`\n  - Sort by weights and reassign in sorted order\n  - Scale to `integral_target`\n  - Clip to [0,1]\n\n3. Fitness evaluation:\n  - Double the input array\n  - Compute full correlation\n  - Find maximum and multiply by `dx`\n  - Return the `C5` value\n\n4. Selection mechanism:\n  - Score based on fitness\n  - Tournament selection (size=3)\n\n5. Crossover and mutation:\n  - Simulated binary crossover (SBX) using eta=10\n  - Polynomial mutation with eta=20\n\n6. Time management:\n  - Use `time.time()` to monitor budget\n  - Cold is 100 seconds = 10,000 evaluations max\n\n7. Output:\n  - `h_values`: Best solution vector\n  - `c5_bound`: Maximum overlap in score format\n  - `n_points`: Used representation size\n\nFallback details:\n- Repair function ensures integral and bounds\n- Explicit fallback repair uses greedy filling\n- Backup includes parent if repair fails",
